<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kenneth Kaunda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kenneth Kaunda was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, from independence in 1964 until 1991. Born at Lubwa mission in northern Zambia, he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a> in the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Central African Federation</a>. He made Zambia a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in 1972, nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines and supported liberation movements in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Economic decline forced multiparty elections in 1991, which he lost to Frederick Chiluba.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda.jpg" alt="Kenneth Kaunda" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenneth_David_Kaunda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kenneth Kaunda was born on 28 April 1924 at Lubwa Mission in Chinsali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, the youngest of eight children. His father, the Reverend David Kaunda, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> missionary and teacher born in Nyasaland, died when Kenneth was a child. His mother, Helen Nyirenda Kaunda, was also a teacher and the first African woman to teach in colonial Northern Rhodesia. He trained at the Munali Training Centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> from 1941 to 1943 and became a teacher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kaunda entered politics in 1949 and in 1953 became Secretary General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-nkumbula">Harry Nkumbula</a>. Kaunda broke with Nkumbula and founded the Zambian African National Congress in October 1958. It was banned in March 1959 and he served nine months in prison. He was elected president of its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a> (UNIP), in January 1960. UNIP won the elections of January 1964, and on 24 October 1964 he became the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p><p>The new state had a very poorly developed school system, with about a hundred university graduates, and Kaunda invested heavily in education; the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> opened in 1966. In April 1968 the Mulungushi Reforms began to bring foreign-owned companies under national control, and a number of mining firms were nationalised. From 1973 rising oil prices and falling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> prices cut state income and left Zambia in debt to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>. Kaunda banned all parties except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">UNIP</a>, and the Choma Declaration and a new constitution made Zambia a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>. He promoted an ideology he called Zambian Humanism.</p><p>He supported the movements against white minority rule in Rhodesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, allowing groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> to operate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzania-zambia-railway">TAZARA Railway</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, completed in 1975, gave Zambia a trade route that did not cross white-ruled territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After riots in 1990 and a failed coup attempt, Kaunda yielded to demands for multiparty politics. In the elections of 1991 Frederick Chiluba of the Movement for Multiparty Democracy won 75 percent of the vote to his 24. Kaunda handed power over on 2 November 1991, the second mainland African head of state to leave office peacefully after losing a free multiparty election. He retired from politics in 1998 and later worked against the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiv-aids">HIV/AIDS</a>. He died on 17 June 2021, aged 97, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> observed 21 days of national mourning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kenneth Kaunda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As independence approached, Litunga Mwanawina III and <b>Kenneth Kaunda</b> signed the Barotseland Agreement on 18 May 1964, bringing Barotseland into Zambia with guarantees for its institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leaders included two former teachers, Harry Nkumbula and <b>Kenneth Kaunda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, the Anglican priest Trevor Huddleston, who had worked in Sophiatown until 1956, became president of the British Anti-Apartheid Movement, and Julius Nyerere of Tanzania and <b>Kenneth Kaunda</b> of Zambia gave the exiled movements bases.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere of Tanzania, Sékou Touré of Guinea, Modibo Keïta of Mali, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, Haile Selassie of Ethiopia and <b>Kenneth Kaunda</b> of Zambia shaped the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Most African one-party states adopted multi-party constitutions in the early 1990s, and in Zambia in 1991 <b>Kenneth Kaunda</b>’s party lost power in a multi-party election.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chinsali, in what is now <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-10.53,32.05&amp;year=1987" aria-label="Chinsali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5433 2557 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kenneth Kaunda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5883 2827h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5883 2827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-10.53,32.05&amp;year=1987">What was here in 1987? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
